  <h2>What we look at when “ads don’t work”</h2>
  <p>
    Nine times out of ten there is a plain reason, not some mystery. First we ask how long the
    campaigns actually ran and what the last agency promised, because a lot of accounts get switched
    off before Google has finished learning. Then we check the website. If people who find you for
    free are not picking up the phone, paying for more visitors will not help. A slow or confusing
    site is the quiet killer behind most “ads don’t work” stories.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then we go through the account itself: half-empty ads, a single tired ad instead of a few being
    tested, keywords far too broad for the budget, no list of searches to block so you are paying
    for people looking for jobs or free advice, and whether Google is being told to chase the right
    kind of call. For most electrical work we would rather cast a wide net than let Google hunt for
    the cheapest, smallest jobs, because a homeowner types “electrician near me”, not the name of
    the repair they need.
  </p>

  <h2>When we turn spend up or down</h2>
  <p>
    Budget follows how busy you are and how much more work you want next month. More spend does not
    automatically mean more booked jobs, sometimes it just means Google gets paid more, so we lift it
    in steps and watch whether the calls are still the kind you want.
  </p>

  <h2>What about Facebook for customers?</h2>
  <p>
    Usually not as your main source. Someone scrolling Facebook was not looking for an electrician,
    so those leads need a lot more chasing. Google catches people at the moment they need you.
    Facebook does earn its place for big-ticket work people think about for months, like generators,
    for reminding past visitors you exist, and most of all for hiring.
  </p>
